course: dictionary
lesson: 4755
title: One word, two jobs
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word hound can be a noun or a verb, and it means different things in each job. As a noun, a hound is a dog used for hunting, with a strong sense of smell. As a verb, to hound someone means to chase or bother them again and again. The words around it show you which job it is doing.
explain: «That is how we got sheepdogs that herd, terriers that dig after rats, retrievers that bring back birds from water, hounds that follow a scent and greyhounds built to sprint.» The hounds here follow a scent, so they are hunting dogs. The clue is the word scent, which is about smell.
explain: «She hounded her mum for a phone.» Hounded is a verb here. Try the swap: she pestered her mum for a phone, or she kept chasing her mum for a phone. Both make sense, so hounded means pestering again and again.
explain: Informal talk is looser. Hound can mean any dog, and a «news hound» is a person who is very keen on news. A swap test helps: if «news hound» becomes «news lover» and the sentence still makes sense, you have the right meaning.
fact: Hound (noun) means a dog used for hunting, such as a foxhound or bloodhound, with a strong sense of smell.
fact: Informally, hound can mean any dog.
fact: Hound (verb) means to chase or bother someone again and again, as in «hounded by reporters».
fact: Long ago, «hound» meant any dog, like German «Hund» (dog) today.
fact: A «news hound» is a person who is very keen on something, here news.
exercise: In «The hounds followed the scent through the wood», what are the hounds doing? | Building a den | Sleeping by the fire | *Tracking the trail by smell | Chasing a ball in the park
exercise: Mum says, «Stop hounding me about the phone!» What does hounding mean? | Cooking dinner | Hiding the phone | *Pestering again and again | Playing a game
exercise: Tom points at a dog and says, «Look at that hound!» What might he mean? | A detective in a story | A cloud shaped like a dog | *Any dog | A hunter's horn
test: Which sentence uses hound as a noun? | They hounded the new shop all day. | Hound me later, please. | *The hound sniffed the old boot. | She hounded him for the answer.
test: Lily is a «news hound» who reads the paper every morning. What does news hound mean? | A dog that sniffs newspapers | *A person very keen on news | A reporter who never sleeps | A newspaper seller
exam: Grandad says, «My old hound hounds me for biscuits every day.» Which two meanings are used? | Two dogs and a bone | *A hunting dog, and to pester again and again | A dog and a cat, and to chase | Any dog, and a news lover
